WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.948
<v Alba>Two's complement only stores whole numbers. A navigator needs fractions,

2
00:00:06.948 --> 00:00:12.867
<v Alba>like latitude 56.1875°. For real numbers, computers use floating point.

3
00:00:13.517 --> 00:00:18.554
<v Alba>The idea in denary: move the point to the front, and count the hops.

4
00:00:18.754 --> 00:00:28.412
<v Alba>Five hops to the left: 34 006.8 = 0.340068 × 10⁵

5
00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:38.726
<v Alba>Computers do the same in binary: 66.625 = 1000010.101

6
00:00:38.926 --> 00:00:43.224
<v Alba>Hop the point left until it sits just before the first 1.

7
00:00:43.224 --> 00:00:45.962
<v Alba>Seven hops, so the exponent is 7.

8
00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:52.904
<v Alba>Store three parts. First, the sign bit: 0 for positive, 1 for negative.

9
00:00:53.104 --> 00:00:58.086
<v Alba>The mantissa: the digits after the point, padded with zeros to 15 bits.

10
00:00:58.286 --> 00:01:02.754
<v Alba>And the exponent: 7, stored in 8-bit two's complement.

11
00:01:03.404 --> 00:01:06.337
<v Captain>And what about a tiny number, Lieutenant?

12
00:01:06.537 --> 00:01:13.191
<v Alba>Then the point hops right and the exponent is negative.

13
00:01:13.191 --> 00:01:19.127
<v Alba>0.0010101 needs two hops right: −2 = 1111 1110.

14
00:01:19.777 --> 00:01:26.963
<v Alba>The total number of bits is fixed. More bits for the mantissa: more precision.

15
00:01:27.163 --> 00:01:34.024
<v Alba>More bits for the exponent: more range. You can't have both.

16
00:01:34.674 --> 00:01:40.911
<v Alba>Mantissa → precision. Exponent → range. Use those exact words in the exam.

